why_this_one:
It is the whole of the client's Meteorite. ZzzCharacter.cpp's damage arm, in the switch on the skill the packet carried:

    case AT_SKILL_METEO:
        CreateEffect(MODEL_FIRE, to->Position, to->Angle, o->Light);
        PlayBuffer(SOUND_METEORITE01);
        break;

Two lines, one model, one sound. Read at a glance that is 'a fire effect on the target' and it is not - see lift_from, which is where the rock actually appears.

MODEL_FIRE is used by eight sub-types across the game and only sub-type 0 is a Meteorite; the numbers on this asset are that branch's and no other's. The Dark Wizard's second skill and the Lich's only one are the same two lines, so building it for the Lich builds it for the wizard.

glow_from:
The mover's shared tail, which runs every frame the rock is alive:

    Vector(Luminosity * 1.f, Luminosity * 0.1f, Luminosity * 0.f, Light);
    AddTerrainLight(o->Position[0], o->Position[1], Light, 2, PrimaryTerrainLight);
    CreateParticleFpsChecked(BITMAP_FIRE, o->Position, o->Angle, Light, 5);

A deep orange-red at two terrain tiles of radius, and the same vector goes to the ground light and to every ember it lays down behind it. Luminosity is re-rolled by MoveEffect every frame as `(rand() % 4 + 7) * 0.1` - 0.7 to 1.0 - and fades over the last five frames of life, so the whole thing shimmers rather than glowing steadily.

The colour belongs to the light and the embers and to nothing else. The flame cone is tinted BodyLight x BlendMeshLight, and BodyLight is the vector CreateEffect was handed - the caster's own light, near white in daylight. Multiplying the cone by the orange as well crushes an already dark-red texture toward black, and what survives is its flame pattern read as banding.

impact_from:
The mover's landing, which for sub-type 0 is `Position[2] < RequestTerrainHeight(...)`:

    o->Position[2] = Height;
    Vector(0.f, 0.f, 0.f, o->Direction);
    Vector(o->Position[0], o->Position[1], o->Position[2] + 80.f, Position);
    for (int j = 0; j < 6; j++)
        CreateEffectFpsChecked(MODEL_STONE1 + rand() % 2, o->Position, o->Angle, o->Light);
    CreateParticleFpsChecked(BITMAP_EXPLOTION, Position, o->Angle, Light);
    o->Live = false;

Six stones off the two models, one blast eighty units up, and the rock gone. The stones are thrown from the floor and the blast from above it, which is what puts the fire at the height of whatever was standing there.

`Light` on that last line is a local vec3_t that nothing on this path ever assigns - declared at the top of MoveEffect and left uninitialised - so the blast's colour in the real client is whatever was on the stack. There is no value to port and the sheet is painted orange and yellow already, so it is drawn untinted. Recorded rather than quietly picked, because it is the one number in this file that is not MU's.
